 "tastes": "<p>Hefe means yeast and weizen means wheat, which describes the beer exactly. At least half the grist is wheat malt. The beer is served with its yeast, so it pours cloudy with a tall white head. All the fruit and spice comes from a special Bavarian yeast strain, not from anything added.</p><p>That yeast makes isoamyl acetate, which smells of banana. It also makes 4-vinyl guaiacol, which smells of clove. The brewer steers the balance with fermentation temperature and pitching rate: warmer and lighter on yeast pushes banana, cooler pushes clove. A good one has both, with a soft bread note from the wheat and almost no hop bitterness.</p><p>Bavarians drink it at lunch, with white sausage and pretzels. It is one of the most consistent styles in the world. It also has a short shelf life. Those delicate esters fade after a few weeks, so fresh really is best.</p>",
